Exercise 2 — if / else if / else
Starting with a &lt;- 3, build up a conditional statement step by step:

Write an if/else block that prints "a is positive" when a &gt; 0 and "a is negative" otherwise.
Extend it with an else if branch so that "a is zero" is printed when a == 0.
Test your code with a = 0, a = 5, and a = -3 by changing the first line.

